Article 55
Special protection for children who are victims of terrorist networks as referred to in Article 41 letter k shall be carried out through efforts to:
a. provide education about education, ideology, and nationalism values;
b. provide counseling about the dangers of terrorism;
c. provide social rehabilitation; and
d. provide social assistance.

Article 64
(1) The provisions as referred to in Article 63 paragraph (1), may be exempted by taking into account the following requirements: a. age for child workers in informal sector work is above 15 (fifteen) years;
b. written approval is obtained from the parent/guardian of the child worker in informal sector work;
c. the implementation must be set forth in a written work agreement between the company/employer and the parent/guardian of the child worker in informal sector work;
d. not employed at night;
e. working time of at most 3 (three) hours per day;
JDIH Kota Singkawang
f. not employed in a place or environment that can disrupt the child's growth and development process, whether physical, mental, moral and intellectual or the child's health;
g. provide opportunities to socialize with family and the surrounding environment;
h. provide opportunities to obtain education in accordance with their talents and interests;
i. employed for light types of work; and j. provide health protection for children and the opportunity for one day off per week.
(2) Protection for child workers in informal sector work as referred to in paragraph (1), aims to: a. prevent all forms of exploitation, discrimination, harassment and violence against children; and
b. protect children from activities that can disrupt the child's growth and development process, whether physical, mental, moral and intellectual or the child's health.
(3) Protection efforts for child workers in informal sector work as referred to in paragraph (2), include among others: a. providing education to the community about children's rights; b. providing assistance in the form of psychological, medical and legal services to child workers in informal sector work who experience exploitation, discrimination, harassment and violence; c. empowering families through the provision of skills training and reduction of expenditures;
d. providing scholarships to child workers in informal sector work who have dropped out of school to continue formal education to a higher level; and e. providing non-formal education and skills training for child workers in informal sector work who do not pursue formal education.

